Literal integer conversion selection — 2026-10-16

Lowering selects sext/zext/trunc of literal bits<1..64> values as constants.
It masks the source before extending and masks the result at its own width.
Dynamic parameters, poison, symbolic addresses and 128-bit values keep the
existing path. No EvoIR operation, guard, effect or EqSat rule is removed.
A literal conversion to true does not qualify as a canonical true obligation.

Targeted tests cover 1,764 width/value/target evaluations, all narrow sign
boundaries, truncation high bits, a constant-valued incoming edge and the
canonical-guard boundary. Existing selection and proof-required tests pass.
Combined acceptance on October 18 passes 456 Rust suites: 3460 passed,
zero failed, eight ignored, plus Clippy, formatting and all six Lean steps.
The first full run had one stale ABI snapshot failure. All 18 changed hashes
were reproduced against retained old assembly and their diffs reviewed:
literal extensions and three immediate stores change; ABI placement, calls,
frames and guard branches do not. The failed run remains in workspace.log.
The passing combined logs are in .evidence/reused-address-20261018/.

Unchanged reductions, original kernel flags and 100,000 calls per case:
Symbol           Instructions/call before after GCC14   Function bytes before after
theta                              195   195      94                    824   824
pte_offset_map                      61    55      23                    312   288
folio_state                         32    32       9                    152   152
Three container executions and three TCG runs pass with the prior checksums.
The TCG runs use the retained GCC kernel, disjoint executable addresses and
zero dropped/irregular blocks. The GCC counts are retained, not newly rerun.
The library-only reductions have no main: their --check refusals are retained,
not counted as successful executed-path checks. N09/N10's 2x exits stay open.

Three original hot units compile and assemble, without installation or boot:
Unit                       Whole object bytes before after
mm/page_alloc.o                               527104 524992
arch/arm64/kvm/mmu.o                            703048 701832
crypto/sha3_generic.o                          253720 253712
These are object bytes, not the S1 .text metric. Each probe retains the same
41 disclosed option omissions; none is unreviewed.

Rejected experiment: merging ordinary label continuations preserves the
tested results but raises theta from 195 to 238 instructions and adds a frame.
Local address reuse extends live ranges across the merged block. That change
and its one-block test expectation were withdrawn; no name-based exception
or block-size threshold was added. Its first capture remains separate.

Capture: .evidence/linear-constants-20261016/, maximum 500 MiB.
casts-only/counts.json records the accepted variant; reductions/counts.json
records the rejected joined-block variant. Original-unit probe records use
the literalcasts suffix in .evidence/live-range-holes-20261016/.
The six hashed compile-only .s/.o probe files (11,398,896 bytes) are retired
after a no-open-handle check. Exact intermediate files must be regenerated;
all logs, measurements, original inputs and current kernel runtimes remain.
Small reduction object files are also retired after hashing and checking
open handles; the combined retirement list is in the October 18 capture.
Disk accounting is combined with kernel-reused-address.out, since both
changes share the same target cache and final acceptance run.
Claim ceiling: bounded ordinary lowering, not a new certified rewrite,
whole-kernel acceptance, performance parity or a clean release build.
